def z(n,key,new,why,s,option=None):qe(q,'virt-container-primitives-q'+str(n),key,new,why,s,option)
e('Every container runtime, from Docker to Kubernetes, ultimately makes the same few system calls. Learn them and containers stop being magic.','These are common primitives for native Linux process containers. Kubernetes orchestrates containers through runtimes; VM-backed and userspace-kernel sandboxes can implement a different boundary.','Kubernetes notruntime/allruntimessamenativepathfalse.',[oci])
e('| user | UIDs, GIDs, capabilities | 3.8 (2013) |','| user | UIDs, GIDs, capabilities | 2.6.23; completed in 3.8 |','Usernamespacesfirstintroducedbefore3.8.',[us])
e('Three system calls do all the work:','Core namespace-management interfaces include:','NamespaceAPIalsoioctl,clone3and/proc;notallworkonlythree.',[ns])
e('- `unshare(flags)` moves the calling process into new namespaces.','- `unshare(flags)` creates requested namespaces; PID and time namespaces apply to subsequently created children rather than changing the caller\'s current membership.','PID/timeunshareexception.',[pid,'https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/time_namespaces.7.html'])
e('- `setns(fd)` joins an existing namespace, via a file in `/proc/<pid>/ns/`. That is how `docker exec` and `nsenter` get "inside" a running container.','- `setns(fd, nstype)` can join an existing namespace through a namespace descriptor. PID membership is different: it selects the namespace for future children, so a helper must also fork. Runtime exec also sets up root, credentials and resource/security controls.','MissingsecondargumentandPIDfork/rootsetup.',[st])
e('PID 1 also gets no default signal handling: a `SIGTERM` is ignored unless the program installed a handler. This is why `docker stop` sometimes waits 10 seconds and then sends `SIGKILL`, and why tools like `tini` exist.','Namespace init has special signal protection: an ordinary SIGTERM needs an installed handler; an ancestor namespace can force SIGKILL/SIGSTOP. Docker\'s stop signal and grace period are configurable (the documented Linux default timeout is 10 seconds). Init helpers also forward signals and reap children.','NotallPID1signalhandlingabsent;stopconfigurable.',[pid,'https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/stop/'])
e('Runtimes create a **veth pair** (a virtual cable), put one end inside, attach the other to a bridge on the host and add NAT rules.','A common bridge configuration creates a **veth pair**, places one end inside and optionally uses host NAT. Host networking, routed networking and other plugins differ.','NotuniversalvethbridgeNAT.',[ns])
e('so "root" in the container is nobody special outside.','so it does not gain initial-user-namespace root privileges merely by being UID 0 inside. It can still access resources allowed to the mapped host identity.','Namespacedrootnotzeroexternalrights.',[us])
e('> They hide things; they do not stop a privileged process from reaching around them. A container\'s root user can still load kernel modules or remount file systems unless capabilities and seccomp remove that power. Every container shares one kernel, and that kernel is the attack surface.','> Namespaces enforce resource isolation, but the overall boundary depends on credentials, capabilities and other controls. Loading host kernel modules requires privileges in the initial user namespace; root in a child user namespace does not acquire that power. Native Linux process containers share a kernel attack surface.','Namespacesnotmerelycosmetichiding;usernsroot cannotmodprobe.',[ns,us])
e('In cgroups v2 (the default in current Fedora, Ubuntu 21.10+, Debian 11+ and RHEL 9) there is a single hierarchy mounted at `/sys/fs/cgroup`, and every controller works in that one tree.','Cgroups v2 uses a unified hierarchy, commonly mounted at `/sys/fs/cgroup`. Inspect the actual host: available/enabled controllers and boot configuration vary.','Removeunverifieddistributiondefault/versionclaims.',[cg])
e('It is all files. Creating a cgroup is `mkdir`; putting a process in it is writing its PID:','A simplified administrator example on a disposable, correctly delegated cgroup v2 hierarchy follows. It assumes the controllers are available, permissions allow these writes, and a service manager is not managing the same subtree. Commands must succeed before continuing; do not treat this as a universal host setup script.','Originalprivilegedrootwritesnotalwaysvalidandexternalmanagerconflict.',[cg])
e('The shell, and every child it starts, is now limited to half a CPU, 256 MiB of memory and 100 processes.','After successful writes, the shell and later children share a fair-scheduler CPU quota averaging half a CPU, a 256 MiB accounted-memory limit and a 100-task limit (threads count). Ancestor limits also apply; pre-existing children are not automatically moved with the shell.','Tasksnotprocesses;inheritedlimitsnotexistingchildmigration.',[cg])
e('| `memory.max` | Hard limit; above it, reclaim then OOM kill |','| `memory.max` | Accounted-memory limit; reclaim/OOM handling |','OOMnotallallocationpaths;temporaryovershoot.',[cg])
e('| `memory.high` | Soft limit; above it, throttle and reclaim hard |','| `memory.high` | Throttling/reclaim threshold; may be exceeded |','Notstrictsoftlimit.',[cg])
e('Four busy threads use the 50 ms in 12.5 ms of wall time and are then throttled for the rest of the window.','In an idealised case with four threads running continuously on four available CPUs, no burst credit and no tighter ancestor limit, 50 ms of budget lasts 12.5 ms of wall time. Scheduler accounting and runtime slices make actual traces less exact.','Needfourcores/assumptionsnotfourthreadsautomaticallyparallel.',[cg])
e('A cgroup with weight 100 on an idle machine can use every core.','A sufficiently parallel group may use otherwise-idle allowed CPUs, subject to quota, affinity, cpuset and ancestor restrictions.','Weightnotbypassotherconstraints.',[cg])
e('the kernel reclaims cache before OOM-killing, but a container can look "full" when it is mostly cache.','reclaimable cache can reduce pressure, but pinned/dirty data and other charges matter. memory.max can be exceeded temporarily, and some allocation failures do not invoke the OOM killer. A container can look "full" when much usage is reclaimable cache.','CachealwaysreclaimedandOOMalwaysfalse.',[cg])
e('In v2, a cgroup that has controllers enabled for its children cannot itself hold processes (the root cgroup is exempt). Processes live in leaves.','For domain resource distribution, a non-root cgroup must have no internal processes before enabling domain controllers for children. The root and threaded-controller subtrees have exceptions; "processes only in leaves" is not a universal rule.','Threadedsubtreesexception.',[cg])
e('The host\'s file system is still mounted, just not reachable by name.','Existing mounts remain, and inherited descriptors or a working directory outside the new root can still provide access.','Chrootdoesnotnecessarilyremovebynamepaththroughcwd.',[pv])
e('Container runtimes instead use **`pivot_root(new_root, put_old)`** inside a new mount namespace:','A common native-runtime setup uses **`pivot_root(new_root, put_old)`** inside a mount namespace, with suitable private mount propagation and no leaked external references:','Notallruntimespivot_rootmandatory.',[pv,oci])
e('Now the host\'s root file system is **not mounted at all** in the container\'s mount namespace. There is nothing to escape to by walking `..`.','This removes the old root from the visible mount tree when setup is correct. It is not a complete security proof: open descriptors, other bind mounts, propagation and retained privileges must also be controlled. Lazy unmount can leave referenced mounts alive.','Unmountnotrevokeopenreferencesorcompleteboundary.',[pv])
e('Copying a 200 MB image for each of 50 containers would waste 10 GB and seconds of start-up. Instead, runtimes stack read-only image layers and give each container a thin writable layer on top, using **overlayfs**:','As a decimal-unit illustration, fifty full 200 MB copies require 10 GB before metadata. Layer sharing can avoid that duplication. One common Linux implementation is **overlayfs**; storage backends vary.','Removeunmeasuredstartupsecondsanduniversalstoragebackend.',[ov])
e('workdir=/c1/work \\\n /c1/merged','workdir=/c1/work \\\n /c1/merged','Placeholder', [ov]) if False else None
# Shell backslash must join mount options into one argument; no indentation after the continued comma.
e('upperdir=/c1/upper,workdir=/c1/work \\\n /c1/merged','upperdir=/c1/upper,workdir=/c1/work \\\n /c1/merged','Placeholder',[ov]) if False else None
e('- **workdir**: scratch space overlayfs needs on the same file system as upperdir.','- **workdir**: initially empty scratch directory on the same filesystem as upperdir; backing filesystem support and non-overlapping paths must meet overlayfs requirements.','Documentmountpreconditions.',[ov])
e('The whole file is copied to upperdir first, then changed.','A file-level upper copy is created before data modification. Metadata-only copy-up, sparse extents and backing-filesystem cloning can alter the actual I/O and physical allocation.','Logicalcopyupnotalwaysphysicalfullcopy.',[ov])
e('(a character device with number 0/0)','(for example, a 0/0 character device or a supported zero-size file with a whiteout xattr)','Currentoverlayregularfilewhiteoutalternative.',[ov])
e('Copy-up is per file, not per block. Change one byte of a 2 GB file from the image and the first write copies all 2 GB. Write-heavy data (databases, logs) belongs in a **volume**, a bind mount from the host that bypasses the overlay.','Copy-up is file-level, so a small change to a large lower file can be expensive; physical copying/allocation depends on sparse and clone support. Write-heavy data often belongs on suitable persistent storage outside the image overlay. Docker-managed volumes and bind mounts are distinct storage choices, not synonyms.','Copyupcostandvolumedefinitioncorrected.',[ov,'https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage/'])
e('A minimal "container", in the order a runtime does it:','A conceptual native-container setup (real runtimes synchronise steps and may delegate storage/network setup):','Notuniversalruncsequence.',[oci])
e('2. Parent: create a cgroup, write limits, write the child\'s PID to `cgroup.procs`; create a veth pair and move one end into the child\'s net namespace.','2. Parent: keep the child blocked while configuring UID/GID maps when needed, cgroup membership/limits and the selected networking.','Preventunsandboxedchildracingconfiguration.',[oci,us])
e('3. Child: mount overlayfs for the root, `pivot_root` into it, mount a fresh `/proc`, set the hostname.','3. Child/runtime: configure the prepared rootfs and mounts, switch root, mount suitable procfs and set the hostname. Rootfs preparation need not use overlayfs or be done by runc.','runcnotimagestoragebackend.',[oci])
e('`memory.max` a hard limit that ends in OOM kills.','`memory.max` an accounted-memory limit with reclaim and allocation/OOM handling.','NotalllimitpathsOOMkill.',[cg])
e('- `chroot` is escapable; runtimes use `pivot_root` in a mount namespace and unmount the old root.','- `chroot` alone is not a security boundary. A root-mount switch plus correct mount, descriptor and privilege isolation is a common stronger setup.','Notallchrootnecessarilyescapablewithoutprivileges.',[pv])
e('with whole-file copy-up and whiteouts for deletes.','with file-level copy-up and whiteouts for deletes; actual storage/I/O costs depend on backing support.','Summarisequalifiedcopyup.',[ov])
z(1,'prompt','Two containers have separate network namespaces and each binds the same local address and TCP port 80, without reuse-port sharing. Which namespace separates these bindings?','WithoutassumptionscoulddifferentIPs/reuseport.',[ns])
z(1,'workedExample','Network namespaces provide independent network stacks and port spaces. The same address/port can therefore be used in each. External access may use routing, a proxy or host-port mappings; NAT to distinct host ports is one option, not a requirement.','ExternalreachabilitynotmustNAT.',[ns])
z(2,'prompt','Which kernel feature provides an accounted-memory limit of 512 MiB for a container group?','NotabsoluteeveryphysicalRAMbytecap.',[cg])
z(2,'explanation','Correct. memory.max is the main cgroup memory limiting mechanism. It covers accounted memory, allows some temporary overshoot and has reclaim/OOM or allocation-failure behavior.','Clarifyhardlimitsemantics.',[cg],0)
z(4,'prompt','In an idealised fair-scheduler model, cpu.max is 25000 100000 with no burst credit or tighter ancestor limit. Four busy threads run continuously on four available CPUs from the period start. Roughly when is the quota exhausted?','Fourthreadsnotfourcoresautomatically;stateidealisednoaccountingovershoot.',[cg])
z(5,'prompt','An ordinary dense 1 GB lower-layer file is appended to through overlayfs. Assume no sparse, compression, reflink or other physical-copy optimisation. Roughly how much upper data is created on the first write?','Physicalallocationambiguouswithoutassumptions.',[ov])
z(5,'workedExample','Under the stated ordinary-copy assumptions, data copy-up creates an upper copy of the 1 GB file, then appends 10 bytes. That is roughly 1 GB of upper file data, while the lower copy remains. Filesystem cloning or sparse allocation can change physical space and I/O costs; the question excludes those optimisations.','Noalloverlayalwaysdoublephysicalallocation.',[ov])
z(6,'explanation','Correct. A whiteout hides the lower name without deleting its data; supported representations include a 0/0 character device or a whiteout xattr on a zero-size file.','Notonlychardevice.',[ov],3)
z(7,'explanation','Correct for an ordinary SIGTERM under this assumption. Namespace init has special signal rules; ancestor-namespace SIGKILL/SIGSTOP are exceptions.','Notalldefaultsignalactionsignored.',[pid],1)
z(7,'text','It should reap children it inherits; some orphans may instead be adopted by a nearer child subreaper','SubreapersbeforePID1.',[pid],2)
z(7,'explanation','Correct. Orphans may go to a live subreaper; otherwise namespace init adopts them. Unreaped terminated children can remain zombies.','Subreaperexception.',[pid],2)
z(7,'workedExample','Namespace init exiting causes namespace teardown. An ordinary SIGTERM requires a handler; stop signal and timeout are runtime-configurable. Init must reap children it inherits, although a nearer subreaper can adopt orphans first. Correctly mounted procfs shows processes visible in that namespace, not all host processes.','Signal/timeouts/subreapersqualified.',[pid])
z(8,'prompt','Why can a properly configured root-mount switch with pivot_root and old-root unmount be stronger than chroot alone?','Notallruntimesuniversal.',[pv])
z(8,'text','It can remove the old root from the visible mount tree, while chroot alone leaves mounts and external references intact','Noabsoluteescapeproof.',[pv],2)
z(8,'explanation','Correct. It improves mount isolation, but inherited file descriptors, bind mounts, propagation and remaining privileges still need control.','Unmountnotcompleteproof.',[pv],2)
z(8,'workedExample','chroot changes path resolution\'s root without resetting the working directory or closing descriptors. A process retaining suitable privileges and outside references may escape that restriction. pivot_root plus unmount can detach the old root from the visible tree, but lazy unmount does not revoke open references. Secure setup must address those too.','Correctnotmountedatallandroot-onlytooabsolute.',[pv])
z(9,'prompt','A container has memory.max=512M, about 150 MiB heap, and substantial clean reclaimable page cache after scanning a file. Assume other charges remain small and reclaim succeeds. Which behavior explains memory.current staying near the limit without a kill?','Originalpredictsalwaysreclaimbasedonlyheapinsufficient.',[cg])
z(9,'explanation','Correct under the stated assumptions: reclaimable clean cache can be dropped. Anonymous memory is not the only possible source of unreclaimable pressure.','AnononlyOOMfalse.',[cg],1)
z(9,'explanation','The stated scenario has successful reclaim, so a failed read is not implied. Allocation or I/O failures are still possible in other conditions.','Readsneverfailfalse.',[cg],2)
z(9,'workedExample','Memory accounting includes anonymous, file-cache and other kernel charges. Here the question explicitly assumes sufficient clean reclaimable cache and successful reclaim, so continued reading can recycle cache without an OOM kill. Actual systems require memory.stat and memory.events; heap size alone does not guarantee reclaim success.','Removereclaimalwayssucceeds.',[cg])
z(10,'text','The runtime prepares a helper in the selected namespaces, forks as needed for PID namespace entry, sets root/credentials/resource controls and executes sh','SetnsPIDcallerunchangedneedsfork.',[st],3)
z(10,'explanation','Correct. Namespace membership is part of runtime setup; joining a mount namespace alone does not set the process root, and PID setns selects the namespace for future children.','setnsonlyinsufficient.',[st],3)
z(10,'workedExample','A runtime helper obtains namespace handles and uses supported namespace-entry operations. PID namespace entry requires creating a child after selection. It also sets the container root, credentials, cgroup and requested security configuration before executing the command. The container application does not need an SSH server or an exec-command protocol.','Correctcompleteconceptualflow.',[st,oci])
z(11,'text','A sufficiently parallel group with weight 10 may use otherwise-idle allowed CPUs if no other quota, affinity or ancestor restriction prevents it','Otherrestrictionsexist.',[cg],1)
z(11,'explanation','Correct. Low relative weight alone does not cap idle CPU utilisation. Available parallelism and other restrictions still apply.','Notallcoresautomatically.',[cg],1)
z(11,'text','A non-root domain cgroup must be empty of internal processes to distribute domain resources to children','Threadedexceptions.',[cg],3)
z(11,'explanation','Correct for domain controllers. Threaded subtrees and the root have exceptions, so this is not a blanket rule for every controller and cgroup.','Correctexceptions.',[cg],3)
z(11,'workedExample','Weight is relative among eligible competitors; it does not reserve fixed cores or override other restrictions. pids.max limits tasks, including threads. The no-internal-process constraint applies to domain-resource distribution with root/threaded exceptions. V2 uses a unified hierarchy rather than one separate tree per controller.','Consistentanswers.',[cg])
